    famousQuote: 'Soldiers of my Old Guard: I bid you farewell! For twenty years I have found you constantly on the road to honor and glory. Do not mourn my fate; if I have consented to survive you, it is to serve your glory: I will write of the great deeds we have done together!',
    summary: 'The heartbreaking valedictory farewell of Napoleon to the veteran soldiers who had marched with him from the sands of Egypt to the snows of Moscow and the gates of Madrid. Having abdicated the imperial throne following the allied capture of Paris, Napoleon embraced the imperial eagle standard and kissed its silken folds amidst weeping soldiers.',
    historicalContext: 'Faced with the sixth coalition’s overwhelming invasion of France and the desertion of his marshals (Ney, Marmont), Napoleon signed the Treaty of Fontainebleau, abdicating unconditionally in favor of exile to the tiny Mediterranean island of Elba.',
    fullExcerptText: `Soldiers of my Old Guard: I bid you farewell! For twenty years I have accompanied you on the road to honor and glory. In these latter times, as in the days of our prosperity, you have never ceased to be models of bravery and fidelity.

With men such as you, our cause was not lost; but the war would have been interminable; it would have been civil war, and France would only have become more unhappy! I have therefore sacrificed all our interests to those of our country; I depart.

As for you, my friends, continue to serve France. Her happiness was my only thought; it will always be the object of my wishes! Do not mourn my fate; if I have consented to survive you, it is to serve your glory: I will write of the great deeds we have done together!

Adieu, my children! I would wish to press you all to my heart; but I will at least press your standard! (He embraces General Petit and kisses the Imperial Eagle). Farewell once more, my old companions! Let this last kiss pass into your hearts!`,
